// spec: how deep constants nest is counted as if nothing had been computed before: Sum<1100>::value is no constant (1100 nested, past the bound of 1024) though Sum<25>, Sum<50>, ... Sum<1075> are asked first, each 25 deeper than the last, and Sum<1000>::value is one; a cached value keeps how deep its own computation went, and an asker nests that much deeper, so no answer depends on what another walk or thread happened to compute first. C++ compilers count only instantiations not made yet: clang compiles this file in this order, not in the reverse one; kpp's declarations have no order
// (master's kpp at -j 1: Sum<1100>'s own value checked as a constant, read from its cache; its errors at -j 1, 8 and 32 were 10, 115 and 187 for the same functions in the reverse order)
// kpp: -j 1
// error: the constexpr variable value of Sum<1100> is no constant
// error: the constexpr variable value of Sum<1025> is no constant
// error: the constexpr variable value of Sum<1024> is no constant
template<int N> struct Sum { static constexpr int value = N + Sum<N - 1>::value; };
template<> struct Sum<0> { static constexpr int value = 0; };
int f1() { constexpr int v = Sum<25>::value; return v % 1000; }
int f2() { constexpr int v = Sum<50>::value; return v % 1000; }
int f3() { constexpr int v = Sum<75>::value; return v % 1000; }
